Setting (pp. 71-73): M is a set of 'cells' or 'figures'. P is a set of points. φ: M → 𝔓(P), X ↦ |X|° (the 'open part' of the realization of X), is a finer invariant than X ↦ |X|. By hypothesis the apartness relation ∥ on M is induced from x ≠ y on P: X ∥ Y ⇔ |X|° ∩ |Y|° = ∅. Grothendieck extends φ to parts A ⊂ M by φ(A) = ∪_{X∈A} φ(X), so that A ∥ B ⇔ φ(A) ∩ φ(B) = ∅. For A ⊂ M he forms the polar co-ψ°(A) = {X ∈ M : X ∥ A} = {X : φ(X) ⊂ P∖φ(A)}. The 'closed' parts are the polars S = co-ψ°(A), and Σ_M is the set of such S. On p. 71 he proves the key identity X ∈ S ⇔ φ(X) ⊂ φ(S).

This page draws the consequence. The map Σ_M → 𝔓(P), S ↦ φ(S) = |S|°, is injective. A left inverse is Q ↦ {X ∈ M : φ(X) ⊂ Q}, which returns S when Q = φ(S). Thus the lattice of ∥-closed parts of M embeds into the power set of P.

He then hesitates: rather than all X with φ(X) ⊂ Q, perhaps take only the minimal elements, or some 's-pr°' operation applied to that set, S_Q = s-pr°({X : φ(X) ⊂ Q}). The natural next question is which parts Q ⊂ P lie in the image, i.e. which point-sets 'are' figures. The garbled lines appear to discuss:
- maximality;
- 'éliminations' that are not compatible;
- dimension and subdivisions;
- limits to particular configurations.
They conclude with the term '"constructibles"' for the corresponding figures.

The margin sketch is a lens-shaped 2-cell, hatched, with a segment and a marked point. It records why the open parts |X|° are needed rather than the closed realizations: two sets of cells can have A ∩ B = ∅ (in M) while |A| ∩ |B| ≠ ∅ (in P), e.g. a cell and a face of it. With |·|° the intersection is empty.

Aim: an axiomatic, point-free 'analysis situs' (tame topology). Figures are defined combinatorially via an apartness or orthogonality relation on cells and then compared with point-set images. The goal is to characterize the 'constructible' subsets of P as images of closed parts of M. The next page continues with supp°X and the condition |X|° = |supp° X|°.
